    "turn_text": "atomic demolition mission or what we commonly call a suitcase nook now how did i learn about this and i was the one that broke the story back in nineteen ninety seven uh... yeltson was running for reelection as president of the of russia and he had two candidates opposing him one was the communists of gone off and the other was a retired two-star general And this retired two-star general was named Alexander Levitt, very popular. Yeltsin knew if Levitt stayed in the race, he would pull enough votes away from Yeltsin that the communists would probably win. So he had to get Levitt out of the race. So he offered him his top security advisor job. So Levitt left the race, went to work for Yeltsin for over a year as his national security advisor. Yeltsin won re-election. Libid was for one year in charge of all security for the entire country of Russia. Well, obviously after Yeltsin won, he eventually got rid of Libid. And that was when I was taking my delegation to Moscow. It was May of 97. We sat in Libid's office and I said, general, tell us about your military. Libid's a former prize fighter, huge man, gigantic fist, talks very slowly and deliberately. He said, Congressman Weldon, our countries and militaries in total disarray. Our best war fighters, our best Soviet generals and admirals, have left the service of the country. And they've left the service of the country because they're not being paid, because morale's terrible, because they're not being given benefits, and soldiers are committing suicide, and these generals and admirals feel betrayed by the motherland. So they're selling off the technology that we developed during the Cold War to use against you, and they're selling that technology to your enemy. And boy, how right he was. Then he went on, and he said, let me give an example. When I was at Yeltsin's side, he tasked me to account for all of the small atomic munitions that we had produced. He said, I knew the figure was 132. And he said, we knew that was the number that we had to account for. He said, as Yeltsin's personal advisor, we searched all of our Ministry of Atomic Energy facilities, all of our military vases, all of our strategic forces command. And I could only find something like 50, which meant that there were 80 unaccount for. And I said, General Levitt, where are the rest? And he looked at the delegation and he shrugged and he said, I don't know. So they",
